1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
 Welcome to this lesson on Antigravities AI models and their limitations.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,840
 In this session, we'll look at what powers Antigravity behind the scenes.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:13,760
 We'll also discuss the practical boundaries you should be aware of when using AI-assisted development tools.

4
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:18,000
 Understanding both strengths and limits helps you work more effectively.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,240
 Antigravity is powered by large language models, often called LMs.

6
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:28,320
 These models are trained on massive data sets that include code documentation and natural language.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,760
 They are designed to understand instructions and respond in ways that help developers.

8
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:38,880
 In practice, they can generate code, fix bugs, and explain complex logic in simple terms.

9
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:42,720
 These models act as intelligent agents throughout your workflow.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,960
 From planning tasks to execution, they provide the reasoning layer behind the tool.

11
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:49,600
 Everything you see Antigravity do starts here.

12
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,360
 Large language models excel at understanding developer intent.

13
00:00:53,840 --> 00:01:00,320
 You can describe what you want in natural language and the model translates that into actionable tasks.

14
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:05,440
 They are strong at writing code across languages like JavaScript and Python.

15
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:09,760
 They can also break down complex features into step-by-step plans.

16
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:15,040
 Testing and verification are supported by simulating behavior and checking results.

17
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:20,400
 Another strength is debugging and refactoring across multiple files.

18
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:25,120
 These capabilities make LMs powerful assistance when used thoughtfully.

19
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:28,320
 Despite their power, AI models do have limits.

20
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:31,360
 They don't always get things right on the first attempt.

21
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:33,760
 Wake instructions can lead to misunderstandings.

22
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:38,640
 Sometimes the generated code doesn't match your projects, structure, or expectations.

23
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:41,520
 Syntax errors can also appear occasionally.

24
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:45,040
 Recognizing these limits helps you stay in control.

25
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:49,520
 The goal is to work with the AI, not assume it's always correct.

26
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:53,120
 There are a few common challenges you should keep in mind.

27
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:58,320
 Wake instructions often cause the AI to make assumptions that don't match your intent.

28
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:03,360
 Missing contexts can lead the model to guess about project structure or conventions.

29
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:08,640
 While usually accurate, syntax mistakes can still happen and need review.

30
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:14,160
 Structural mismatches may appear if architecture details aren't clear.

31
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:19,840
 These issues aren't failures, but signals that more guidance is needed.

32
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,440
 Being specific goes a long way toward better results.

33
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:28,000
 AI models have a limited context window which acts like working memory.

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,080
 This means they can only see a certain amount of code at one time.

35
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:36,160
 In large projects, important details from earlier files can be missed.

36
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,080
 To work around this, it helps to break tasks into smaller pieces.

37
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,600
 Point the AI to specific files or functions when possible.

38
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:47,200
 Providing short summaries of relevant context also helps.

39
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,800
 Always review outputs carefully to ensure consistency.

40
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:55,440
 It's important to understand that LLMs predict code rather than execute it.

41
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:01,280
 They generate code based on patterns they've learned not by running it like a compiler.

42
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:05,280
 This means code can look correct but behave differently in practice.

43
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:09,600
 The model doesn't truly test logic in your environment.

44
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:12,320
 That's why human testing is still essential.

45
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:16,000
 Treat AI generated code as a strong draft not a final answer.

46
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,240
 Validation remains your responsibility.

47
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,880
 Antigravity works best with human oversight.

48
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:23,920
 You review and approve plans before execution begins.

49
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:27,200
 Generated code should be examined for quality and correctness.

50
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:30,080
 Testing outcomes in your own environment is critical.

51
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:33,760
 Feedback helps guide the AI toward better results over time.

52
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,640
 Think of the AI as a capable junior developer.

53
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,680
 It's fast and helpful, but it still needs direction.

54
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,120
 See you in the next lesson.

